From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 12:01:25 +0200
Subject: MIPS: branch: read the branch instruction with a mode-aware accessor

__compute_return_epc() fetches the branch whose delay slot faulted with a
bare __get_user(). On an EVA kernel that expands to lwe, which performs the
load with user access rights whatever mode the CPU is in, so it raises an
address error for any kernel-range address. The only address this function
ever hands it is regs->cp0_epc -- kernel text, whenever the fault being
unwound came from the kernel.

Every kernel-mode unaligned access in a branch delay slot is therefore
fatal on EVA rather than emulated. do_ade() decodes the access and reaches
compute_return_epc() to step past the branch; delay_slot() is set, so
__compute_return_epc() runs and its lwe takes a nested AdEL on the very
kernel text it was reading. The recursive do_ade() decodes that lwe as a
spec3 lwe_op, and that arm of emulate_load_store_insn() exists only for a
kernel accessing user memory, so it applies access_ok() unconditionally,
rejects the kernel address and dies:

  Unhandled kernel unaligned access[#1]:
  epc   : 8050d1f4 __compute_return_epc+0x18/0x68
  ra    : 80513b20 do_ade+0x410/0x854
  Cause : 00800010 (ExcCode 04)
  BadVA : 85f8b19c
  Code: 30a20003  1440000c  00000000 <7ca5002f> 14400003  8fbf0014

7ca5002f is "lwe a1,0(a1)", and BadVA is the cp0_epc of the outer fault --
the branch this function had been asked to read. Captured on a MIPS
interAptiv (GRX350) with CPU_MIPS32_3_5_EVA; the outer unaligned access was
in module code and would have been emulated in silence on a non-EVA kernel.

unaligned.c had the same problem and lost it in commit 45deb5faeb9e ("MIPS:
uaccess: Remove get_fs/set_fs call sites"), which replaced the surrounding
set_fs(KERNEL_DS) with the mode-aware helpers in access-helper.h; its own
instruction fetch is now __get_inst32(&insn.word, pc, user). branch.c reads
an instruction for the same reason from the same exception path and was not
converted with it. Use the helper here as well, so a kernel-mode fetch goes
through get_kernel_nofault() and only a user-mode fetch reaches the EVA
accessors. The failure path is unchanged.

The user-mode side does change slightly: __get_inst32() calls get_user()
where this used to call __get_user(), so a user-mode cp0_epc now passes
an access_ok() check before the fetch and returns -EFAULT rather than
issuing an unchecked lwe if it is out of range. That matches what
unaligned.c already does with the same helper, and cp0_epc is exception
state rather than anything the caller vouched for, so the check is the
right way round.

Nothing changes for non-EVA builds beyond the pagefault_disable()
pairing inside copy_from_kernel_nofault(): there user_lw() is
kernel_lw() already, so both sides of __get_inst32() were emitting the
same lw with the same exception-table fixup.

Fixes: 45deb5faeb9e ("MIPS: uaccess: Remove get_fs/set_fs call sites")
Cc: stable@vger.kernel.org
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/arch/mips/kernel/branch.c b/arch/mips/kernel/branch.c
index 0216ff24c392..2a6ba5012e5a 100644
--- a/arch/mips/kernel/branch.c
+++ b/arch/mips/kernel/branch.c
@@ -20,6 +20,7 @@
 #include <asm/ptrace.h>
 #include <linux/uaccess.h>
 
+#include "access-helper.h"
 #include "probes-common.h"
 
 /*
@@ -848,7 +849,6 @@ EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(__compute_return_epc_for_insn);
 
 int __compute_return_epc(struct pt_regs *regs)
 {
-	unsigned int __user *addr;
 	long epc;
 	union mips_instruction insn;
 
@@ -859,8 +859,7 @@ int __compute_return_epc(struct pt_regs *regs)
 	/*
 	 * Read the instruction
 	 */
-	addr = (unsigned int __user *) epc;
-	if (__get_user(insn.word, addr)) {
+	if (__get_inst32(&insn.word, (u32 *)epc, user_mode(regs))) {
 		force_sig(SIGSEGV);
 		return -EFAULT;
 	}
